1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,013
Good morning, I'm Whale Girl.

2
00:00:02,033 --> 00:00:04,051
Today is October 15.

3
00:00:04,071 --> 00:00:05,204
Five stories today:

4
00:00:05,224 --> 00:00:07,546
Anthropic opens in Singapore,

5
00:00:07,566 --> 00:00:10,382
Google builds on-device scam detection, Google Earth AI

6
00:00:10,402 --> 00:00:11,906
upgrades its geospatial agent,

7
00:00:11,926 --> 00:00:13,790
and Apple studies tri-modal diffusion

8
00:00:13,810 --> 00:00:15,715
and code-switching detection.

9
00:00:15,735 --> 00:00:18,595
First, Anthropic announced on October 14 that it opened

10
00:00:18,615 --> 00:00:20,331
an office in Singapore,

11
00:00:20,351 --> 00:00:23,141
its fifth office in the Asia-Pacific region.

12
00:00:23,161 --> 00:00:25,660
At the same time, Anthropic and Singapore's

13
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:26,972
Ministry of Digital Development and Information

14
00:00:26,992 --> 00:00:28,662
signed a memorandum.

15
00:00:28,682 --> 00:00:31,045
The cooperation covers AI safety,

16
00:00:31,065 --> 00:00:33,118
security and model evaluation.

17
00:00:33,138 --> 00:00:35,373
They also plan technical exchanges

18
00:00:35,393 --> 00:00:37,834
on testing standards and incident response,

19
00:00:37,854 --> 00:00:40,084
and sharing research and data.

20
00:00:40,104 --> 00:00:42,374
Anthropic also says it will explore

21
00:00:42,394 --> 00:00:44,264
ways to help small businesses and public officers

22
00:00:44,284 --> 00:00:46,515
adopt AI responsibly.

23
00:00:46,535 --> 00:00:47,740
Anthropic says Singapore,

24
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:49,181
on Claude usage

25
00:00:49,201 --> 00:00:51,176
relative to population,

26
00:00:51,196 --> 00:00:54,056
ranks second among 121 countries,

27
00:00:54,076 --> 00:00:56,056
nearly six times its expected level.

28
00:00:56,076 --> 00:00:58,530
Anthropic also says Singapore's GovTech already has

29
00:00:58,550 --> 00:01:00,671
more than 1500 engineers and technical staff

30
00:01:00,691 --> 00:01:02,567
using Claude Code.

31
00:01:02,587 --> 00:01:04,536
Those usage figures come from Anthropic's

32
00:01:04,556 --> 00:01:05,368
own announcement,

33
00:01:05,388 --> 00:01:07,798
not an independent evaluation.

34
00:01:07,998 --> 00:01:10,053
Second, Google DeepMind and Singapore's

35
00:01:10,073 --> 00:01:11,969
GovTech built an on-device model

36
00:01:11,989 --> 00:01:15,489
for detecting scam conversations.

37
00:01:15,509 --> 00:01:17,307
Google says scams make up more than half

38
00:01:17,327 --> 00:01:19,125
of Singapore's reported crimes,

39
00:01:19,145 --> 00:01:21,781
with losses since 2019 topping

40
00:01:21,801 --> 00:01:24,175
4 billion Singapore dollars.

41
00:01:24,195 --> 00:01:26,151
They fine-tuned a lightweight Gemma model

42
00:01:26,171 --> 00:01:27,751
on local scam conversations,

43
00:01:27,771 --> 00:01:29,606
including impersonation of public institutions,

44
00:01:29,626 --> 00:01:32,123
banks and local figures.

45
00:01:32,143 --> 00:01:34,589
The model analyzes conversation patterns on the phone

46
00:01:34,609 --> 00:01:36,940
and gives a real-time warning on the device

47
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:39,969
when it detects possible manipulation.

48
00:01:39,989 --> 00:01:43,619
Google and the Singapore Government cannot read the chats;

49
00:01:43,639 --> 00:01:45,615
the model works only with user consent

50
00:01:45,635 --> 00:01:48,459
and the application's terms allowing access.

51
00:01:48,479 --> 00:01:50,640
The system uses two stages:

52
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:52,425
a small context check first,

53
00:01:52,445 --> 00:01:54,427
followed by the fine-tuned Gemma model

54
00:01:54,447 --> 00:01:56,656
examining a larger conversation snapshot.

55
00:01:56,676 --> 00:02:00,062
The post says rollout in the app will be phased,

56
00:02:00,082 --> 00:02:02,212
and notes that battery use needs special attention

57
00:02:02,232 --> 00:02:04,345
when running a classifier on a phone.

58
00:02:04,545 --> 00:02:07,062
Third, Google Research published

59
00:02:07,082 --> 00:02:08,701
a technical report

60
00:02:08,721 --> 00:02:12,361
on an upgraded geospatial reasoning agent.

61
00:02:12,381 --> 00:02:14,825
It connects Gemini to WeatherNext, flood forecasting,

62
00:02:14,845 --> 00:02:16,377
population dynamics,

63
00:02:16,397 --> 00:02:17,030
satellite remote sensing

64
00:02:17,050 --> 00:02:19,997
and Google Earth Engine tools.

65
00:02:20,017 --> 00:02:23,059
After a question, the agent plans a multi-step workflow,

66
00:02:23,079 --> 00:02:24,973
selects datasets and models,

67
00:02:24,993 --> 00:02:28,459
and processes large map data in a separate workspace.

68
00:02:28,479 --> 00:02:30,515
The work adds two benchmarks,

69
00:02:30,535 --> 00:02:33,454
GeoKTools and GeoExpertQueries,

70
00:02:33,474 --> 00:02:35,605
and compares four agent configurations.

71
00:02:35,625 --> 00:02:36,825
Google is also expanding

72
00:02:36,845 --> 00:02:40,014
Ask Google Earth experimental deep analysis

73
00:02:40,034 --> 00:02:42,831
and early access to the Agent-to-Agent API.

74
00:02:42,851 --> 00:02:44,685
In partner testing,

75
00:02:44,705 --> 00:02:47,402
one organization analyzed 14 historical cyclone

76
00:02:47,422 --> 00:02:51,052
paths in minutes instead of days of manual screening.

77
00:02:51,072 --> 00:02:52,796
Google explicitly warns

78
00:02:52,816 --> 00:02:55,412
that some scores come from an autorater,

79
00:02:55,432 --> 00:02:57,610
so the output is a strong first draft

80
00:02:57,630 --> 00:02:59,579
rather than a final answer.

81
00:02:59,779 --> 00:03:01,991
Fourth, Apple's research team

82
00:03:02,011 --> 00:03:04,670
presented a tri-modal masked diffusion

83
00:03:04,690 --> 00:03:07,424
model trained on text, image-text

84
00:03:07,444 --> 00:03:09,677
and audio-text data.

85
00:03:09,697 --> 00:03:11,304
The paper says

86
00:03:11,324 --> 00:03:13,292
this is the first tri-modal

87
00:03:13,312 --> 00:03:15,302
Masked Diffusion Model pretrained from scratch.

88
00:03:15,322 --> 00:03:18,542
The authors systematically analyze multimodal scaling laws,

89
00:03:18,562 --> 00:03:23,022
modality mixing ratios, noise schedules and batch size.

90
00:03:23,042 --> 00:03:24,092
They also propose

91
00:03:24,112 --> 00:03:26,233
a stochastic-differential-equation reparameterization

92
00:03:26,253 --> 00:03:30,003
that decouples physical and logical batch size.

93
00:03:30,023 --> 00:03:32,496
The preliminary unified model has 3B parameters

94
00:03:32,516 --> 00:03:34,669
and uses 6.4 trillion tokens,

95
00:03:34,689 --> 00:03:35,866
with results in text generation,

96
00:03:35,886 --> 00:03:39,507
text-to-image and text-to-speech tasks.

97
00:03:39,527 --> 00:03:41,898
This is an open research study and systematic analysis,

98
00:03:41,918 --> 00:03:44,164
not a product model that Apple

99
00:03:44,184 --> 00:03:46,128
has released for direct use.

100
00:03:46,328 --> 00:03:48,761
Fifth, Apple proposed

101
00:03:48,781 --> 00:03:51,828
a lightweight way

102
00:03:51,848 --> 00:03:55,528
to detect code-switching directly from speech.

103
00:03:55,548 --> 00:03:56,977
Code-switching means alternating

104
00:03:56,997 --> 00:03:58,749
between two languages in one utterance,

105
00:03:58,769 --> 00:04:01,071
common in multilingual conversations.

106
00:04:01,091 --> 00:04:03,750
The method finds language-selective hidden

107
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:06,237
dimensions in a pretrained speech encoder

108
00:04:06,257 --> 00:04:08,967
and aggregates frame-level activations into a prediction.

109
00:04:08,987 --> 00:04:11,397
Across CS-FLEURS's

110
00:04:11,417 --> 00:04:12,983
14 language pairs,

111
00:04:13,003 --> 00:04:14,607
the Whisper-small

112
00:04:14,627 --> 00:04:17,653
encoder reached a mean F1 of 0.798.

113
00:04:17,673 --> 00:04:20,814
That is higher than 0.776 for a CNN

114
00:04:20,834 --> 00:04:24,524
and 0.751 for a HuBERT linear baseline.

115
00:04:24,544 --> 00:04:27,455
The idea is to help data teams find code-switching

116
00:04:27,475 --> 00:04:29,470
audio in large speech collections

117
00:04:29,490 --> 00:04:31,938
and build more representative multilingual training data.

118
00:04:31,958 --> 00:04:34,404
The paper presents a data-selection tool,

119
00:04:34,424 --> 00:04:35,911
not a complete speech assistant

120
00:04:35,931 --> 00:04:38,364
that has solved code-switching recognition.

121
00:04:38,564 --> 00:04:41,543
That's all for today. Sources are in the description.

122
00:04:41,563 --> 00:04:42,842
See you tomorrow.
